# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clautolisp/alfe: named encoding "situations", per-situation CLI options, and line-termination
#+DATE: 2026-07-27

* Status

*VALIDATED 2026-07-28 (pjb).* The situation list is settled at SIX:
=source=, =file=, =console= (CAD GUI console device), =cadstdio= (CAD
process OS pipes), =log=, =terminal= (alfe/clautolisp's own). Arbitration
points a–d resolved (see §3): one =file=; two encodings (in/out) on
=console=/=cadstdio=/=terminal= (+ read/write on =file=); =-e= and =-E=
DROPPED; the console-area three-way split (=console= ≠ =cadstdio= ≠
=terminal=). The option set is §4. Downstream still to complete: the §7
defaults table (needs experiments E1–E5, several CAD-blocked) and the §6
plumbing — but implementation of the settled situations + CLI can start.
Key implementation constraint (§2.1): all =load= implementations
(=builtin-load=/=autolisp-load-file-in-context= engine, =autolisp-source-load=
CAD-side shadow — CONFIRMED still present, currently NOT encoding-aware =
the macOS bug, =direct-load= alfe bridge, and the external =tu:load=) MUST
share one algorithm, signature, and encoding-parameter processing for the
=source= situation.

Supersedes the "To design (pjb)" section of
=alfe-cad-console-encoding.issue=, which sketched a per-backend console
option only. The scope here is wider: there is not one console stream to
configure but a handful of distinct *situations*, each with its own
encoding, each fixed or configurable depending on the CAD and its
version.

* 1. Why

Today `-E` is parsed, threaded into `start-engine`, and then dropped:

    backend-bricscad.lisp:599   (declare (ignore host mock-input dialect dwg load-encoding io-encoding))
    backend-autocad.lisp:552    (declare (ignore dialect host mock-input load-encoding io-encoding))
    backend-clautolisp.lisp:389 (declare (ignore mock-input bootstrap-phase io-encoding mode dwg))

and the one place that decodes CAD console output takes no encoding at
all — `decode-console-octets` (file-protocol.lisp:564) is a fixed
cascade: UTF-16LE by BOM/heuristic, then UTF-8 BOM, then lenient UTF-8
with a per-byte `(code-char b)` fallback, i.e. Latin-1. So the only two
outcomes reachable are UTF-16LE and Latin-1; no third encoding can be
requested, and there is no parameter to request it through.

Consequence measured on BricsCAD V26 / macOS (alfe 1.6.14): the CAD
writes its console in Mac Roman, the fallback decodes Latin-1, and `é`
(Mac Roman 0x8E) reaches the transcript as U+008E. Identical output for
`-E MAC-ROMAN`, `-E WINDOWS-1252` and `-E UTF-8`.

The reverse direction is *fine* and must not regress: a literal `é`
passed in `-x` arrives in BricsCAD as one character of code 233,
independently of `-E`.

* 2. What we know, per situation × CAD × version × setting

Provenance is marked: [doc] vendor documentation, [obs] measured here,
[?] unknown, to be measured.

** 2.1 AutoLISP source files (`load`, alfe `-l`)

| CAD / setting              | Encoding                                   | Configurable?                      |
|----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+------------------------------------|
| AutoCAD, LISPSYS 0         | MBCS / ASCII, "no encoding argument"       | via LISPSYS only; restart required |
| AutoCAD, LISPSYS 1 (dflt)  | Unicode support in AutoLISP functions      | via LISPSYS only; restart required |
| AutoCAD, LISPSYS 2         | Unicode support, MBCS when *compiled*      | via LISPSYS only; restart required |
| BricsCAD, any              | UTF-8 *if BOM present*, else ANSI/codepage | no per-call setting                |
| BricsCAD V26 / macOS       | "ANSI" is Mac Roman                        | no                                 |
| clautolisp                 | alfe `-e ENC` (honoured, incl. line-ending suffix) | yes                        |

[doc] LISPSYS documents editor choice, Unicode support and the encoding
used when *compiling* LSP files. It does **not** state how a `.lsp` is
*decoded at load time*. → experiment E1.

[obs] BricsCAD in-engine `(load …)` ignores alfe's `-e`; that is
`cad-load-encoding-macos.issue`, and it is the same axis as this issue:
the source situation has no per-engine setting to forward `-e` into.

Our load implementations (verified 2026-07-28) — the `source` situation
spans ALL of them, and they MUST share one algorithm, signature, and
encoding-parameter processing (a fourth, macro-load, may join):
- `builtin-load` → `autolisp-load-file-in-context`
  (=CL/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:546=, runtime :2829) —
  in-process engine; HONOURS the encoding. **The reference algorithm.**
- `autolisp-source-load` (=AFE/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp:563=,
  family 432/461/566, dispatch `autolisp-eval-load-form` :688, registered
  `*CLLOAD_DEFAULT_LOADER*` :833) — the CAD-side shadow that deports the
  program's I/O over the protocol. **CONFIRMED still present; native
  `(open … "r")` with NO encoding ⇒ host codepage (macOS Mac Roman). This
  is the bug.** Must be brought to the reference algorithm; watch the
  pure-ASCII constraint on the vendored runtime (bootstrap :541-547).
- `direct-load` (=AFE/source/backend-clautolisp.lisp:505=) — the
  alfe→clautolisp bridge; delegates to the engine, OK.
- `tu:load` — the SCHMS+ unit-test loader (external, sncf-reseau/src/schms);
  out of this tree but must follow the same contract.

** 2.2 Files opened by the program (`open`, `read-line`, `write-line`, `princ` to a file)

| CAD / setting             | Default encoding        | Per-call override                          |
|---------------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------------------------|
| AutoCAD, LISPSYS 0        | MBCS                    | none — the `encoding` arg does not exist    |
| AutoCAD, LISPSYS 1 or 2   | MBCS when arg omitted   | `(open f mode "utf8" \| "utf8-bom")`        |
| BricsCAD                  | ANSI / system codepage  | `(open f "r\|w,ccs=UTF-8\|UTF-16LE\|UNICODE")` |
| BricsCAD V26 / macOS      | **Mac Roman, LF**       | as above                                    |

[doc] AutoCAD: `(open filename mode [encoding])`; "When a value isn't
provided for the argument, the file is assumed to contain multibyte
character set (MBCS) which is the legacy behavior"; accepted values
`"utf8"` and `"utf8-bom"` only.

[doc] BricsCAD: `,ccs=<translation>` appended to the mode; `UTF-8`,
`UTF-16LE`, `UNICODE` (= UTF-16LE, read only, auto-adapts). UTF-8 BOM
optional; without BOM the file is treated as ANSI.

[obs] BricsCAD V26 macOS, writing `A` `(chr 233)` `B`:

    (open f "w")                → 41 8e 42 0a     Mac Roman, LF
    (open f "w,ccs=UTF-8")      → 41 c3 a9 42 0a  UTF-8, LF        ✓
    (open f "w,ccs=UTF-16LE")   → 41 c3 a9 42 0a  UTF-8, LF        ✗

  `ccs=UTF-16LE` is *accepted without error* and silently produces UTF-8
  on macOS. Worth a separate vendor-divergence probe.

** 2.3 Console (program's `princ` / `read-line` on stdin-stdout)

| CAD / setting                 | Encoding                      | Configurable?                  |
|-------------------------------+-------------------------------+--------------------------------|
| AutoCAD GUI                   | no accessible console         | n/a                            |
| accoreconsole 2022 FR         | **UTF-16LE both ways**, no BOM on input | **no known setting** |
| BricsCAD GUI                  | no accessible console         | n/a                            |
| BricsCAD V26 macOS, batch     | **Mac Roman** out             | [?] no known setting           |
| BricsCAD Windows, batch       | [?] presumably cp1252 or UTF-8 | [?]                           |
| clautolisp                    | alfe `-E` — currently ignored | yes, once plumbed              |

[obs, pjb] `accoreconsole/accoreconsole.org`: input must carry no BOM (a
leading BOM is read as part of the first command); `/l` selects language
not encoding; `SYSCODEPAGE` is read-only ("ANSI_1252") and independent of
the pipe encoding; `DWGCODEPAGE` is its alias; `chcp 65001` has no effect
on UTF-16 units written straight into a pipe. Conclusion: for
accoreconsole the console encoding is **fixed by the product**, so the
option must be a no-op-with-warning there rather than a promise.

Note the line-termination consequence recorded in the same document:
CRLF arriving on accoreconsole stdin counts as *two* Enter events, so the
console situation needs a line-termination setting, not only a charset.

** 2.4 CAD log file (LOGFILEMODE / LOGFILEPATH / LOGFILENAME)

| CAD                    | Encoding | Configurable? |
|------------------------+----------+---------------|
| AutoCAD                | [?]      | [?]           |
| BricsCAD               | [?]      | [?]           |

[?] Not documented by either vendor as far as the search went. Attempted
to measure on BricsCAD V26 macOS in batch mode: setting `LOGFILEPATH` +
`LOGFILEMODE 1` produced no log file, so the measurement failed rather
than yielded a value. → experiment E2.

This situation matters to us concretely: the SCHMS+ harness routes
`(vl-bt)` through LOGFILE on BricsCAD batch because the LISP console is
not redirected there.

** 2.5 alfe / clautolisp terminal

What the *user* sees. Distinct from every row above: it is our own
output stream, not the CAD's. Currently governed by the platform default
and by `--no-color`; `--list-encodings` mentions that the line-ending
suffix is "accepted-and-ignored on -E (terminal IO is line-buffered by
default)".

** 2.6 alfe↔CAD protocol files

`protocol/stdout.txt`, `stderr.txt`, status/request/response. alfe writes
them `:external-format :utf-8` hard-coded (file-protocol.lisp 273/320/359/1372,
backend-bricscad 289/444/488, backend-autocad 379/397) and reads the two
channel files through `decode-console-octets`.

The channel files are the *transport* of situation 2.3 for the file-IPC
backends — that is why `-E` looks like it "should" work there. The
control files are alfe's own and their payload is ASCII.

* 3. Proposed situation list — TO VALIDATE

The name is what the CLI option is built from, so it should read well in
both forms. Proposal:

| # | Situation   | Covers                                                              | Who fixes it                     |
|---+-------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------|
| 1 | `source`    | `.lsp`/`.dcl` read by `load` and by alfe `-l` (ALL load impls, §2.1) | engine (LISPSYS / BOM rule)      |
| 2 | `file`      | files the AutoLISP program opens with `open`                        | per-call arg; default = ours     |
| 3 | `console`   | the CAD's OWN console DEVICE (the GUI text console) — the *true* console; may differ from its stdio | product; [?] likely unsettable   |
| 4 | `cadstdio`  | the CAD subprocess's stdin/stdout/stderr OS pipes (accoreconsole UTF-16LE; BricsCAD Qt on stdout) | fixed-known / Unix `LC_*` default |
| 5 | `log`       | the CAD log file (LOGFILEMODE)                                      | [?]                              |
| 6 | `terminal`  | alfe's / clautolisp's own REPL I/O to the user                     | us (settable)                    |

Console-area distinction (pjb 2026-07-28): three different streams share
the word "console" — the CAD GUI console DEVICE (`console`, product-fixed,
maybe unsettable), the CAD process OS pipes (`cadstdio`, accoreconsole
UTF-16LE / Unix LC_), and alfe's own terminal (`terminal`, ours). What a
probe's `princ` writes lands on `cadstdio` for accoreconsole but on the
file-IPC `protocol` channel for BricsCAD batch; the GUI `console` is only
in the loop with a visible GUI. Situations 3/4/6 each carry TWO encodings
(input + output — §arbitrated-b).

Deliberately *not* proposed as user-facing situations, but say if you
want them:

- `protocol` — alfe's control files. Internal, payload ASCII; exposing it
  invites people to break the protocol. Could be a debug-only option.
- `dwg` / `dxf` — the drawing codec's own string encoding (SYSCODEPAGE /
  DWGCODEPAGE, and libredwg's codepage handling). A real situation, but a
  different subsystem; probably its own issue.

Points ARBITRATED (pjb 2026-07-28):

a. RESOLVED — `file` stays ONE situation (a user wants a consistent
   load/file rule). The read/write divergence (BricsCAD `ccs=UNICODE`
   auto-adapts on read only) is captured in the §7 defaults table, not a
   second option. NB: the cross-CAD `source` discrepancy traces to our
   having THREE+ `load` implementations (see §2.1) — they MUST share one
   algorithm, signature, and encoding-parameter processing.
b. RESOLVED — a console needs TWO encodings, INPUT and OUTPUT. Usually the
   same, but a configuration may differ (e.g. an ISO-8859-1 keyboard with
   a UTF-8 screen), and the line-termination treatment differs by
   direction regardless. So the console/terminal options take an optional
   direction: `-E<situation>[-in|-out]` (bare form sets both).
c. RESOLVED — DROP both `-e` AND `-E` (no legacy aliases,
   per pjb-cli-option-convention).
d. RESOLVED — THREE distinct streams share the word "console" (see the §3
   table + note), so three distinct situations:
   - `console`  = the CAD's OWN console DEVICE (GUI text console). The
                  *true* console; product-fixed, [?] whether settable.
                  Option `-Econsole`.
   - `cadstdio` = the CAD subprocess's OS stdin/stdout/stderr pipes
                  (accoreconsole UTF-16LE; BricsCAD Qt on stdout; Unix may
                  default from `LC_*`). Option `-Ecadstdio`.
   - `terminal` = alfe's / clautolisp's OWN REPL device to the user; ours.
                  Option `-Eterminal`.
   (Current `-E` = terminal I/O per the code-map E5, NOT the CAD console;
   the `-E`=source-file text in alfe-cad-console-encoding is stale.)

* 4. Proposed CLI shape

Per pjb: a clisp-style short form and a long form.

    -E{situation} {encoding}          e.g. -Esource UTF-8   -Econsole UTF-16LE
    --{situation}-encoding {encoding} e.g. --source-encoding UTF-8

(pjb wrote `--{situation}--encoding` with a double dash; assumed a typo,
single dash used above — say if the double dash is intended.)

A bare `-E {encoding}` / `--encoding {encoding}` setting *every*
situation at once would be a convenient shorthand; propose adding it.

** Resolved option set (pjb 2026-07-28) — `-e` and `-E` DROPPED

| situation  | short         | long                       | directions   |
|------------+---------------+----------------------------+--------------|
| `source`   | `-Esource`    | `--source-encoding`        | (read)       |
| `file`     | `-Efile`      | `--file-encoding`          | read / write |
| `console`  | `-Econsole`   | `--console-encoding`       | in / out     |
| `cadstdio` | `-Ecadstdio`  | `--cadstdio-encoding`      | in / out     |
| `log`      | `-Elog`       | `--log-encoding`           | (read)       |
| `terminal` | `-Eterminal`  | `--terminal-encoding`      | in / out     |

Direction (arbitrated-b — two encodings, input and output, may differ):
the bare option sets BOTH directions; a suffix picks one:
`-E<situation>-in` / `-E<situation>-out` (and `--<situation>-input-encoding`
/ `--<situation>-output-encoding`). For `file` the two directions are
`-read` / `-write`. The `{encoding}-{line-termination}` suffix (§5) is
valid on all of them.

Rules:

- Unknown situation name → hard error listing the valid ones, never a
  silent no-op.
- A situation that is fixed by the product for the selected backend ×
  version (accoreconsole console) → accept the option, *warn* that it is
  ignored and why, and keep the product value. Silently ignoring is what
  produced the bug that started all this.
- `--list-encodings` gains a companion `--list-situations` printing the
  table of §3 with, for the selected backend, the resolved default and
  whether it is settable.

* 5. Line termination

Prior art already exists and should simply be generalised: encoding names
accept a suffix, currently `-mac` / `-dos` / `-unix` / `-lf` / `-cr` /
`-crlf`, "honoured on -e (file reads); accepted-and-ignored on -E".

Proposal: `{encoding}-{line-termination}` becomes valid on **every**
situation option, with per-situation semantics:

| Situation | Line termination |
|-----------+------------------|
| `source`  | honoured on read (already is) |
| `file`    | honoured both directions |
| `console` | honoured; **required** for accoreconsole input, where CRLF is two Enter events |
| `log`     | honoured on read |
| `terminal`| honoured on write |

Where the product fixes it, the resolved value must be *reported* by
`--list-situations`, not silently overridden.

* 6. Plumbing — "forwarded and set inside the relevant module"

The spec text must say explicitly that declaring the option is not
enough. Concretely, today:

1. `cli.lisp:884` passes `:io-encoding` to `start-engine` — keep, extend
   to one keyword per situation.
2. Every backend must stop discarding it: remove `io-encoding` from the
   three `(declare (ignore …))` forms listed in §1 and use it.
3. `decode-console-octets` and `drain-channel` must take the resolved
   `console` encoding as an argument. Today neither has a parameter; the
   auto-detect cascade becomes the behaviour of a distinguished
   `AUTO` value rather than the only behaviour.
4. `autocad-console-external-format` (backend-autocad.lisp:442, env
   `$ALFE_AUTOCAD_CONSOLE_ENCODING`) folds into the `console` situation;
   keep the env var as a lower-priority fallback.
5. For the engine-side situations (`source`, `file`, `console` inside the
   CAD), forwarding means emitting the right thing into the CAD:
   `(setvar "LISPSYS" …)` is a restart-level decision, so `source` for
   AutoCAD is a *precondition to check and report*, not something alfe
   can set mid-session. `file` can be forwarded by having the bootstrap
   default the `open` shim's encoding argument / `ccs=`.

Point 5 is the one with real design content: for several situations alfe
cannot *set* the encoding, only *know* it and adapt. The spec should
distinguish three states per (situation, backend, version, platform):
**settable by us**, **fixed by the product and known**, **unknown**.

* 7. Defaults table

Defaults are keyed on (situation, backend, version, **platform**), not on
backend alone — BricsCAD writes Mac Roman on macOS and (presumably)
cp1252 or UTF-8 on Windows. The existing sketch in
`alfe-cad-console-encoding.issue` ("autocad -> ?, bricscad -> utf-8")
is therefore under-specified.

Seed values, to complete as experiments land:

| Situation | clautolisp | BricsCAD/macOS | BricsCAD/Windows | accoreconsole      |
|-----------+------------+----------------+------------------+--------------------|
| source    | `-e`, dflt UTF-8 | Mac Roman unless BOM | cp1252 unless BOM [?] | per LISPSYS [?] |
| file      | UTF-8      | Mac Roman-lf   | [?]              | MBCS               |
| console   | UTF-8      | Mac Roman      | [?]              | **UTF-16LE, fixed, no BOM in** |
| log       | n/a        | [?]            | [?]              | [?]                |
| terminal  | platform default | —        | —                | —                  |

* 8. Experiments

- E1 — AutoCAD: does `(load "x.lsp")` decode by BOM, by LISPSYS, or by
  codepage? Vary LISPSYS 0/1/2 × file with/without UTF-8 BOM × cp1252
  bytes. Needs the Windows/AutoCAD runner.
- E2 — log file encoding, both CADs. First make a log file appear at all
  in batch mode; the BricsCAD/macOS attempt here produced none despite
  `LOGFILEMODE 1` + `LOGFILEPATH`.
- E3 — BricsCAD/Windows console encoding in `-B` batch, to fill the
  column above.
- E4 — BricsCAD `ccs=UTF-16LE` writes UTF-8 on macOS (measured). Confirm
  on Windows; if it differs, it is a platform divergence worth its own
  probe in the conformance suite.
- E5 — confirm the current real meaning of `-e`, `-E` and whether
  `-Eterminal` exists, before assigning aliases (§3.d).

